qualityObjective.en:
For foundation walls, use the same core controls as concrete, but pay additional attention to:
vertical alignment/plumbness;
wall thickness;
penetrations;
waterstops;
construction joints;
openings;
embedded plates;
ties;
waterproofing substrate condition.
The workflow is:
Formwork → Rebar → Embeds → Pre-pour → Concrete → Acceptance
with wall-specific checks layered in.

Key workflow
Subgrade → granular → insulation → vapour barrier → reinforcement → embeds → pre-pour → concrete → finish → joints → curing → acceptance
Critical checks:
subbase acceptance;
slab thickness;
vapour barrier continuity;
insulation thickness;
reinforcement position;
sleeves/drains/embeds;
elevation;
slopes;
floor flatness/levelness if specified;
joints;
curing;
surface defects.
This should eventually become its own full module because slabs can have many interfaces.

Create a continuous, properly terminated and protected waterproofing system before it becomes concealed by insulation/protection/backfill.
Critical items:
substrate condition;
approved membrane;
primer;
temperature/weather;
continuity;
seams/laps;
corners;
penetrations;
terminations;
tie-hole repairs;
membrane damage;
protection board;
pre-backfill inspection.
This is one of the strongest pre-concealment workflows in the entire substructure.

Check:
approved product;
thickness;
location;
continuity;
attachment;
joints;
gaps;
damage;
compatibility with waterproofing;
protection before backfill.

